        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[A NetAddr::IP Trial Release]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[NetAddr::IP has been introduced a beta release 4.080_01 under the guidance of a new maintainer. Vital improvements have been introduced to the module in the new version. Invalid legacy behaviors have been eliminated as well. It’s noteworthy that the testing suite has been fully updated to Test2::V1, thereby getting wider CI coverage for the modern Perl versions.]]></description>
            <pubDate>2026.09.22</pubDate>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://blogs.perl.org/users/dean/2026/09/a-netaddrip-trial-release.html</guid>
            <link>https://blogs.perl.org/users/dean/2026/09/a-netaddrip-trial-release.html</link>
            
            
            
            <author><![CDATA[dean_hamstead]]></author>
            
                
            
            
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[QUERY in Dancer2]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[The post discusses extending the Dancer2 web framework with preliminary support for the new HTTP QUERY method (RFC 10008). It covers some simple changes made to Dancer2's fundamental modules (DSL, Request, Types), an extensive test suite provided through Plack::Test, and working examples of REST API querying.]]></description>
            <pubDate>2026.09.27</pubDate>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://theweeklychallenge.org/blog/query-in-dancer2/</guid>
            <link>https://theweeklychallenge.org/blog/query-in-dancer2/</link>
            
            
            
            <author><![CDATA[mohammad_anwar]]></author>
            
                
            
            
        </item>
        
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[pperl 0.6.20 - The Player of Games]]></title>
            <description><![CDATA[PetaMem's new release, pperl 0.6.20, has brought many performance improvements, achieving up to 7.3 times improvement over node.js according to Benchmarks Game performance metrics due to JIT optimizations including bump-arena tree builds and SIMD intervals proof. The release has also given rise to a 52 times faster Memoize due to compiled cache hits, made possible by the native integration of the 3D graphics with the help of Rust's wgpu crate, and provided a lot of new documentation.]]></description>
            <pubDate>2026.09.27</pubDate>
            <guid isPermaLink="false">https://blogs.perl.org/users/petamem/2026/09/pperl-0620---the-player-of-games.html</guid>
            <link>https://blogs.perl.org/users/petamem/2026/09/pperl-0620---the-player-of-games.html</link>
            
            
            
            <author><![CDATA[petamem]]></author>
            
                
            
            
